Instruction: Read the following excerpt from a merger agreement and answer the multiple-choice question by choosing the option that best characterizes the agreement.\nQuestion: Is the "ability to consummate" concept subject to Material Adverse Effect carveouts?\nOption A: No\nOption B: Yes\n\nMerger Agreement: "Company Material Adverse Impact" means any event, change, circumstance, or condition that, individually or in aggregate with similar events, (A) materially impairs the business, financial condition, operational performance, or long-term prospects of the Company, or (B) hinders, delays, or otherwise impairs the ability of the Company to consummate the transactions contemplated by this Agreement. Provided, however, that none of the following shall be deemed to constitute a Material Adverse Impact: (i) any adverse change solely attributable to general economic or industry-wide conditions affecting all market participants; (ii) any regulatory adjustment or accounting change that affects competitors similarly; and (iii) any delay in consummation resulting exclusively from force majeure events, including natural disasters or mandatory governmental measures, that are beyond the reasonable control of the Company. Notwithstanding the foregoing exclusions, any adverse impact on the Company’s ability to consummate the transaction that arises from specific operational or financial weaknesses, or that is not solely attributable to the general market or external force majeure events, shall be considered under this definition.\n
