Instruction: Read the segment of a merger agreement and answer the multiple-choice question by choosing the option that best characterizes the agreement.
Question: Is the 'ability to consummate' concept subject to Material Adverse Effect (MAE) carveouts?
Option A: No
Option B: Yes

Merger Agreement: "Firm Material Adverse Effect" means any occurrence, change, event or circumstance, whether occurring individually or in aggregate, that would materially impair the operational performance, asset values, or financial condition of the Firm, or that would prevent, materially delay or materially impair the ability of the Firm to consummate the Transaction contemplated herein. Provided, however, that for the purposes of determining a Firm Material Adverse Effect, none of the following circumstances shall be taken into account:
  (i) changes in general economic conditions or industry-wide market fluctuations, including standard variations in interest rates, liquidity or currency exchange rates;
  (ii) modifications in applicable regulatory or legislative frameworks, or general political instabilities affecting the industry as a whole;
  (iii) force majeure events such as natural disasters, pandemics, or significant public health emergencies, except where such events adversely and disproportionately affect the Firm compared to its industry peers;
  (iv) routine variances in the Firm's share price or deviations from its internally projected financial metrics.
It is expressly stipulated that the series of carveout exceptions set forth above do not extend to the provision governing the consummation of the Transaction. That is, even if any or all of these exceptions occur, they shall not mitigate or modify the assessment of any impairment in the Firm's ability to consummate the Transaction as contemplated by this Agreement.

