Instruction: Read the following segment of a merger agreement and answer the multiple-choice question by choosing the option that best characterizes the agreement.

Question: Is the “ability to consummate” concept subject to Material Adverse Effect carveouts?
Option A: No
Option B: Yes

Merger Agreement Excerpt:
“Party Material Negative Impact” means any event, circumstance, or change that (i) would materially impair the ability of the Party to consummate the Merger, or (ii) would have a material adverse effect on the overall business, assets, liabilities, operations, or financial condition of the Party and its affiliates. However, for purposes of prong (i) only, the following shall not be deemed to create a Party Material Negative Impact:
   (1) Fluctuations in market conditions, including moderate variations in interest rates, exchange rates, and short-term liquidity fluctuations;
   (2) Force majeure events, including natural disasters, pandemics, and regional political unrest, even if such events cause minor delays;
   (3) Post-Agreement adjustments to accounting standards or their interpretations;
   (4) Expenditures solely related to post-Effective Date integration efforts, provided such expenditures do not cause a disproportionate effect relative to industry norms;
   (5) The announcement or public disclosure of this Merger;
   (6) Isolated declines in market valuation unaccompanied by verifiable adverse operational performance.
Furthermore, no condition that solely affects procedural timelines or minor administrative aspects of the consummation process—unless aggregated effects meet a threshold specified in Section 9.3—shall be considered as impairing the ability to consummate. 

